<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Thrace</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> is the region between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestos">Nestos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Evros</a> rivers in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>, it saw a short-lived Muslim-led provisional government in 1913, passed to Greece after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and its Muslim minority was exempted from the population exchange of 1923.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-thrace.jpg" alt="Western Thrace" width="320" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SilentResident,Philly boy92 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_of_Thrace_(Greece).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, also called Greek Thrace, is the western part of the wider region of Thrace; the European part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-thrace">Eastern Thrace</a>, and most of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> is Northern Thrace. It is divided into the regional units of Xanthi, Rhodope and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Evros</a>, which with Drama, Kavala and Thasos form the region of Eastern Macedonia and Thrace. It is bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a> to the south and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestos">Nestos</a> and Evros rivers to the west and east. Its largest city is Alexandroupolis. The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a>, an ancient people of Southeast Europe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> colonised the coast and built cities such as Maroneia and Abdera, the home of the philosophers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democritus">Democritus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protagoras">Protagoras</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> took the region in the late 6th century BCE as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satrapy</a> of Skudra, and in the 4th century BCE Philip II of Macedon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquered most of it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> annexed the Sapaean kingdom, and the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracia">Thracia</a> was founded in 46 CE. The region lay near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> conquered most of it in the late 14th century and ruled it until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> of 1912 to 1913, with a mixed population of Greeks, Turks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, and smaller numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomaks">Pomaks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>, Bulgarian troops occupied Western Thrace, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> kept it by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> in August 1913. It had to surrender the region under the Treaty of Neuilly in 1919, and in April 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-remo-conference">San Remo conference</a> gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Greek refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-thrace">Eastern Thrace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> were settled there, and the Greek-Turkish population exchanges of 1923 reversed the earlier population balance. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a> granted the Muslims of Western Thrace the status of a minority. In 1941 Bulgarian troops occupied the area, and some 4,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of the region were arrested and deported, none of whom survived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>About two thirds of the population are Orthodox Christian Greeks and about a third are Muslims, an officially recognised minority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> made up of Turks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomaks">Pomaks</a> and Muslim Romani. The Democritus University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> is based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komotini">Komotini</a>. Archaeological sites include the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> at Maroneia and the sanctuary on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Western Thrace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…its Balkan neighbours, shaped the wars that ended Ottoman rule in Europe, and its territory grew from the narrow state of 1832 to include the Ionian Islands (1864), Thessaly and Arta (1881), Macedonia, Epirus, Crete and the eastern Aegean islands (1912–13), <b>Western Thrace</b> (1920) and the Dodecanese (1947).</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Bulgaria took <b>Western Thrace</b> in the First Balkan War.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-western-thrace">Provisional Government of Western Thrace</a></li></ul></section>
